Fix the low-res tile-edge seam (dark grid over the page while scrolling).

lowResBufferSize() rounded the buffer UP with ceil(), but the content was painted
at the requested scale. At 0.3 a 256 px tile therefore paints 76.8 of a 77-texel
buffer and leaves a transparent 0.2-texel column along the right/bottom edge.
The compositor upscales that buffer ~3.3x back to the full tile rect, so the
unpainted fraction is bilinear-blended into every tile boundary and appears as a
dark seam -- a visible grid across the page whenever low-res tiles are active.

This is NOT specific to the cost trigger: it affects the shipped speed ladder
identically. It was simply easy to miss there, because degradation always ended
and the sharpen-at-rest repaint removed the grid as soon as scrolling stopped.
It is plainly visible mid-scroll on a heavy page (franceinfo.fr).

Fix: size the buffer with lround() (clamped to [1, fullSize]) and paint at
lowResEffectiveScale() = bufferSize/fullSize rather than at the requested scale.
The content then fills the buffer exactly, so there is no unpainted texel to
sample.

Deriving the paint scale from the buffer also keeps tiles registered with each
other. Simply flooring the buffer would remove the transparent column but leave
the paint and composite ratios mismatched (~1% for a 256 px tile at 0.3),
stretching content and misaligning adjacent tiles -- trading a seam for a worse
artifact. Because the compositor upscales the same buffer back to the same rect,
bufferSize/fullSize makes the two ratios exact inverses.

Applied to both raster paths: the synchronous main-thread paint() and the
threaded replay() task (the live path on this device).

Must apply after webkit-lowres-tiles-during-scroll-env.patch,
webkit-lowres-tiles-cpu-path.patch and
webkit-tile-raster-cost-instrumentation.patch (same file).

